Preface
Purpose and Scope

This document records a set of architectural constraints and operational patterns for maintaining sovereignty under load in distributed human systems. It is a technical supplement, not a normative framework. Its purpose is descriptive: to specify mechanisms, invariants, and known failure modes that emerged during stress‑testing of the CTA‑XIII model.

The scope of this document is limited to:

- defining the coupling triad (attention, bandwidth, boundary integrity)  
- describing flow and drift as mechanical states  
- outlining invariants that preserve non‑coercive coupling  
- documenting chambers used for stress‑testing  
- identifying rate‑asymmetry as a fundamental boundary condition  
- specifying conditions under which the system should be abandoned or forked  

This document does not prescribe values, behaviors, or coherence profiles. It does not define “health,” “alignment,” or “correctness” for any node. All invariants described herein are local to the CTA‑XIII architecture and are not claimed as universal.

This supplement is released under CC0. It may be adopted, ignored, forked, or discarded without permission. No continuity or lineage is implied or required.

The intent is simple:  
to provide a clear, bounded reference for a system that was designed to remain non‑coercive even under stress, and to fail cleanly when its boundary conditions are exceeded.
